WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.721
The OT threat landscape is the collection of actors, events, weaknesses, access paths, and operational conditions that can cause harm to industrial and cyber-physical systems.

00:00:11.721 --> 00:00:18.577
It includes deliberate attacks, ordinary malware, insider actions, vendor risk, configuration mistakes, and accidents.

00:00:18.577 --> 00:00:29.414
The landscape is broader than a list of threat groups because operational design and human behavior shape what a threat can actually affect.

00:00:29.414 --> 00:00:33.174
Operational technology monitors or controls physical processes.

00:00:33.174 --> 00:00:40.472
A cyber event may therefore influence production, equipment, safety, environmental conditions, reliability, or public services.

00:00:40.472 --> 00:00:44.674
Loss of a file is a data impact.

00:00:44.674 --> 00:00:51.530
Loss of trustworthy pressure readings, control communications, or operator visibility is an operational impact.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.963
Some incidents create both.

00:00:53.963 --> 00:00:58.607
Threat analysis should connect technical events to process consequences.

00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:04.578
Malware on a business workstation may be contained as an IT event.

00:01:04.578 --> 00:01:12.319
The same malware affecting an engineering workstation may delay maintenance or undermine confidence in controller configurations.

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:21.387
A network outage may stop reporting while local control continues, or it may remove functions required for safe operation.

00:01:21.387 --> 00:01:23.819
Context determines the consequence.

00:01:23.819 --> 00:01:28.906
OT risk also depends on independent safeguards and recovery capability.

00:01:28.906 --> 00:01:36.204
Mechanical protection, safety systems, local operating procedures, segmentation, backups, and trained personnel may limit harm.

00:01:36.204 --> 00:01:47.041
Analysts need to know which protections are independent, whether they remain available during a cyber incident, and how the organization verifies trusted operation.

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:51.685
Understanding the threat landscape is an ongoing operational practice.

00:01:51.685 --> 00:01:59.426
Maintain visibility into assets, architecture, remote access, vulnerabilities, adversary activity, incidents, and changes to the process.

00:01:59.426 --> 00:02:03.628
The objective is not to predict every attack.

00:02:03.628 --> 00:02:12.696
It is to recognize credible pathways, prepare for their consequences, and reduce risk without disrupting the operation being protected.
